NLP and LLM Infrastructure Notebook
This notebook documents the weekly progression of tasks focused on Natural Language Processing (NLP) tokenization comparison, multilingual subword analysis, and local LLM runtime infrastructure setup.

Table of Contents
Week 4: Tokenizer Comparison
Week 5: Multilingual Subword Tokenization with mT5
Week 6: Local LLM Infrastructure Setup (Ollama)
Week 4: Tokenizer Comparison
Objective: Evaluate how different tokenization algorithms process text in various languages.
Models Compared:
BPE (Byte Pair Encoding): Using the gpt2 tokenizer.
WordPiece: Using bert-base-multilingual-cased (M-BERT).
SentencePiece: Using xlm-roberta-base (XLM-R).
Test Languages: English, Telugu, and Hindi.
Key Takeaways: Different tokenizers vary significantly in vocabulary representation, piece length, and overall token counts when dealing with non-Latin scripts.
Week 5: Multilingual Subword Tokenization with mT5
Objective: Deep-dive into multilingual subword-level tokenization using a massive translation model framework.
Model used: Google's google/mt5-small model tokenizer.
Analysis: Demonstrates how multilingual vocabularies use byte-fallback configurations and specific subword tokens (such as prefix marker ▁) to represent complex structures in Dravidian (Telugu) and Indo-Aryan (Hindi) scripts.
Week 6: Local LLM Infrastructure Setup (Ollama)
Objective: Deploy and manage a containerized/local LLM hosting service directly inside the notebook environment.
Steps Implemented:
Dependencies Installation: Update system package indices and set up zstd compression utilities.
Ollama Installation: Automatically fetch and install the official lightweight Ollama binary to run inference locally.
Server Runtime Daemon: Spawn a non-blocking background subprocess to host the Ollama server on localhost:11434 with persistent health-checks via Python.
